"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[71184],{871184:e=>{e.exports="# Unsupported DAX Patterns\n\nThese DAX patterns have no direct UC metric view equivalent. Mark each affected measure `cannot_migrate` with the reason from this file's headers; surface the reason in the final report. The system prompt's failure protocol enumerates the canonical reasons (`complex_filter_context`, `time_intelligence`, `parameter`, `lod_fixed`, `no_skill_rule`, `source_table_unavailable`, `data_not_in_uc`, `unmappable_visual`).\n\n## ALLSELECTED()\n\n```\nDAX:  CALCULATE([Revenue], ALLSELECTED())\n```\n\n`ALLSELECTED` computes a value across all items visible in the current visual context (the slicer/filter scope). There is no UC metric view equivalent because metric views don't have a concept of \"visual context\".\n\n**Reason:** `complex_filter_context`.\n\n**Workaround:** Use coarser LOD with window measures (`range: all`) for simple cases; see `dax-patterns.md` \xa74 for the shape. For complex cases, implement as **dashboard-layer calculations** or **SQL window functions in the source view** (see `source-views.md` for source-view emission).\n\n## USERELATIONSHIP()\n\n```\nDAX:  CALCULATE([Revenue], USERELATIONSHIP(orders[ship_date], calendar[date]))\n```\n\nPower BI supports inactive relationships that can be activated per-measure via `USERELATIONSHIP`. UC metric views have a single join graph — no concept of active/inactive relationships.\n\n**Reason:** `complex_filter_context`.\n\n**Workaround:** Define separate joins for each relationship (`joins[]` entries with distinct aliases), or create separate metric views per relationship path. The user picks which MV to query based on the join semantics they want.\n\n## Time intelligence functions\n\n### SAMEPERIODLASTYEAR\n\n```\nDAX:  CALCULATE([Revenue], SAMEPERIODLASTYEAR('Calendar'[Date]))\n```\n\n**Reason:** `time_intelligence`.\n\n**Workaround:** This is a **point-shifted comparison** (March 2024 ↔ March 2023), not a rolling aggregation. A `trailing 1 year` window is **not** equivalent — that aggregates the prior 12-month range. The correct approaches:\n\n- **Source-view precomputation (preferred).** First aggregate the underlying rows to the comparison grain you want (e.g. one row per `month \xd7 dim_combo`). Then add the prior-year column. Two patterns — **prefer Pattern B unless you can guarantee the pre-aggregated series is dense at the comparison grain**:\n\n  ```sql\n  -- Pattern A: pre-aggregate, densify against a calendar/period spine, THEN LAG.\n  -- LAG walks N rows, not N periods — without densification, a `region` with\n  -- missing months returns the wrong row from `LAG(..., 12)`.\n  WITH monthly AS (\n    SELECT DATE_TRUNC('month', order_date) AS month, region, SUM(amount) AS amount\n    FROM <upstream>\n    GROUP BY 1, 2\n  ),\n  spine AS (  -- calendar \xd7 dimension cross-join; the densification step\n    SELECT m.month, r.region\n    FROM <calendar_months> m\n    CROSS JOIN (SELECT DISTINCT region FROM monthly) r\n  ),\n  dense AS (\n    -- Leave `amount` as NULL on missing periods to preserve Power BI BLANK\n    -- semantics (visuals hide BLANK rows). Replace with COALESCE(..., 0) only\n    -- if the business intent is \"missing = zero\".\n    SELECT s.month, s.region, monthly.amount\n    FROM spine s\n    LEFT JOIN monthly USING (month, region)\n  )\n  SELECT\n    month, region, amount,\n    LAG(amount, 12) OVER (PARTITION BY region ORDER BY month) AS prior_year_amount\n  FROM dense;\n\n  -- Pattern B: shifted self-join keyed by date + relevant dimensions.\n  -- Correct regardless of density — recommended default unless the spine is\n  -- already known to be dense.\n  SELECT\n    cur.month, cur.region, cur.amount AS amount,\n    prev.amount AS prior_year_amount\n  FROM monthly cur\n  LEFT JOIN monthly prev\n    ON prev.region = cur.region\n   AND prev.month = ADD_MONTHS(cur.month, -12);\n  ```\n\n  Then expose `SUM(prior_year_amount)` as a measure.\n\n  **Don't** apply `LAG(amount)` directly to the raw fact-row stream — when there are multiple rows per period (which is the common case), `LAG` walks adjacent rows, not adjacent periods, and the shift is semantically wrong. **Don't** apply `LAG` to a sparse pre-aggregated series either — densify against a calendar spine first (Pattern A) or use the shifted self-join (Pattern B).\n\n- **Skip** with reason `time_intelligence` if the source-view rewrite is not feasible without restructuring the model.\n\nTrailing/rolling windows are correct for \"rolling 12-month sum\" semantics, not for same-period-last-year comparisons.\n\n### DATESINPERIOD / DATESBETWEEN\n\n```\nDAX:  CALCULATE([Revenue], DATESINPERIOD('Calendar'[Date], MAX('Calendar'[Date]), -30, DAY))\n```\n\n**Reason:** `time_intelligence`.\n\n**Workaround:** Use window measures with `trailing 30 day` range.\n\n### TOTALYTD / TOTALQTD / TOTALMTD\n\n```\nDAX:  TOTALYTD([Revenue], 'Calendar'[Date])\n```\n\n**Reason:** `time_intelligence`.\n\n**Workaround:** Use window measures with cumulative range plus a period boundary:\n\n```yaml\nmeasures:\n  - name: ytd_revenue\n    expr: SUM(source.revenue)\n    window:\n      - order: date\n        range: cumulative\n        semiadditive: last\n      - order: year\n        range: current\n        semiadditive: last\n```\n\n### PARALLELPERIOD / PREVIOUSMONTH / PREVIOUSYEAR\n\n```\nDAX:  CALCULATE([Revenue], PARALLELPERIOD('Calendar'[Date], -1, MONTH))\n```\n\n**Reason:** `time_intelligence`.\n\n**Workaround:** Same as `SAMEPERIODLASTYEAR` above — these are **point-shifted comparisons**, not rolling aggregations. Precompute the shifted measure in the source view by **first aggregating to the intended comparison grain** (`SELECT period, dim_combo, SUM(amount) GROUP BY ...`), then prefer the **shifted self-join** on `period = period - <offset>` with the relevant dimensions in the join key. `LAG(amount, <offset>)` is only correct when the pre-aggregated series is dense — densify against a calendar/period spine first (Pattern A above) or use the self-join (Pattern B above). Don't apply `LAG` to raw fact rows. Skip if the source-view rewrite is not feasible.\n\n## EXTERNALMEASURE\n\n```\nDAX:  EXTERNALMEASURE(...)\n```\n\nReferences a measure in an external Analysis Services model. Completely out of scope — requires migrating the AS source to Databricks first.\n\n**Reason:** `data_not_in_uc`. Trigger the 3-way upstream-resolution prompt from `source-views.md` so the user can supply explicit mappings or skip.\n\n## USERPRINCIPALNAME()\n\n```\nDAX:  USERPRINCIPALNAME()\n```\n\nReturns the email of the current Power BI user — used for row-level security gates in DAX measures. No metric view equivalent.\n\n**Reason:** `parameter` (effectively a runtime-resolved parameter).\n\n**Workaround:** Use UC row access policies with `CURRENT_USER()`. This is a separate provisioning step outside the migration agent's scope; surface to the user in the final report.\n\n## ISFILTERED() / ISCROSSFILTERED()\n\n```\nDAX:  IF(ISFILTERED(table[column]), [filtered_measure], [unfiltered_measure])\n```\n\nTests whether a specific column has an active filter in the visual context. Pure display-layer logic — no metric view equivalent.\n\n**Reason:** `complex_filter_context` (display-layer probe).\n\n**Action:** Skip entirely. This is not a metric definition; it's a presentation-layer guard. If the dashboard truly needs this conditional, express it as widget-level conditional rendering in the AI/BI dashboard.\n\n## CONCATENATEX / NAMEOF\n\n```\nDAX:  CONCATENATEX(VALUES(table[col]), table[col], \", \")\n```\n\nString aggregation across rows. Display-layer logic for building filter labels (e.g. \"Showing data for: Region A, Region B\").\n\n**Reason:** `complex_filter_context` (display-layer string aggregation).\n\n**Action:** Skip entirely. The metric view exposes the underlying dimension; the dashboard widget can render selected values via its own filter-display affordances.\n\n## SELECTEDVALUE (as guard)\n\n```\nDAX:  IF(ISBLANK(SELECTEDVALUE(dim[col])), BLANK(), [measure])\n```\n\nReturns `BLANK` when no single value is selected in a slicer. Display-layer guard logic.\n\n**Reason:** `complex_filter_context` (display-layer guard).\n\n**Action:** Skip the guard. The measure itself is still valid — translate the inner `[measure]` and let the dashboard handle the empty-state UX.\n\nFor the **`SWITCH(SELECTEDVALUE(...), ...)`** dispatch pattern (which is structurally different — a real slicer-dispatch toggle), see `dax-patterns.md` \xa75 and `architecture-patterns.md` \xa72.\n\n## LOD / Calculated Table References\n\n```\nDAX:  CALCULATETABLE(...)\nDAX:  SELECTCOLUMNS(...) / ADDCOLUMNS(...)  inside a measure\n```\n\nLOD-style fixed calculations or measures that operate over derived tables don't map cleanly to a single metric-view aggregation. In some cases a SQL window with `PARTITION BY` captures the intent (see `dax-patterns.md` \xa74 for share-of-total via `range: all`); in most others the right answer is structural, not translational.\n\n**Reason:** `lod_fixed`.\n\n**Workaround:** Restructure as a source SQL view with the LOD pre-computed (`SELECT ..., SUM(x) OVER (PARTITION BY ...) AS x_lod FROM ...`), then expose `x_lod` as a column the metric view can aggregate or reference. See `source-views.md` for source-view emission.\n"}}]);
